*adjusts glasses and contemplates the mathematical elegance of a shopping cart*

Ah, a shopping cart implementation. Let me approach this with the rigor it deserves. The tests shall guide us to truth, as a well-constructed proof guides us to a theorem.

```go
package cart

// Item represents a product in the shopping cart
type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

// ShoppingCart holds items for purchase
type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

// New creates a new shopping cart
func New() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

// AddItem adds an item to the cart or increments quantity if it exists
func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(id, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[id] = &Item{
		ID:       id,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

// RemoveItem removes an item entirely from the cart
func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) {
	delete(c.items, id)
}

// UpdateQuantity sets the quantity for an item
func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(id string, quantity int) {
	if item, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		if quantity <= 0 {
			delete(c.items, id)
			return
		}
		item.Quantity = quantity
	}
}

// GetItem retrieves an item by ID
func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) *Item {
	return c.items[id]
}

// GetItems returns all items in the cart
func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

// Total calculates the sum of price * quantity for all items
func (c *ShoppingCart) Total() float64 {
	var sum float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		sum += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return sum
}

// ItemCount returns the total number of items (sum of quantities)
func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

// Clear empties the cart
func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

// IsEmpty returns true if the cart has no items
func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*strokes beard thoughtfully*

You see, the implementation follows directly from what the tests demand—nothing more, nothing less. I have resisted the temptation to add discounts, tax calculations, or inventory validation. As I wrote in 1974, "premature optimization is the root of all evil," and I would extend this to premature featurization.

The map provides O(1) lookup for items by ID—a mathematically sound choice. The struct is simple, the methods minimal. Each function does precisely one thing.

I could spend decades making this more elegant, but the tests require results today. The implementation is correct, and correctness must precede all else.
